{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Titan's Cost Structure, Margins & Working Capital Forecast",
  "scenario": "You are modelling Titan Company Limited's FY25–FY27 financials. The jewellery retailer has grown steadily but faces input-cost inflation and working-capital pressure as it expands stores. Your task: build Titan's cost waterfall (COGS, OpEx, D&A), forecast margins by separating fixed and variable costs, compute operating leverage, and model working-capital days (DSO, DIO, DPO) to derive cash conversion cycle.",
  "instructions": "Build cost-driven margin forecasts, operating-leverage ratios, and working-capital day metrics for Titan across three forecast years, starting from historical actuals. Each answer must use formulas that reference the Data sheet, not hard-coded numbers.",
  "learning_objective": "Forecast profit margins by modelling cost structure (variable and fixed components separately), compute operating leverage and margin expansion, and derive working-capital requirements (receivables, inventory, payables) using days-based metrics that drive cash flow.",
  "hints": [
    "EBITDA = Revenue − COGS − OpEx. To find the margin, divide by Revenue.",
    "Variable costs scale with revenue; use a percentage assumption (e.g. 65% of revenue). Fixed costs are a separate lump; add them to the variable total.",
    "DSO = Receivables ÷ Revenue × 365. If you forecast Receivables for FY25, use the FY25 Revenue and the DSO formula rearranged: Receivables = DSO/365 × Revenue."
  ],
